## Normal form and ZR/ZF scope

Use a contact-minimal, then diameter-pair-minimal triangle-free global minimizer and retain every source point, including contact-isolated points. Let `V` be the nonisolated positive-contact core and `v=|V|`.

The canonical §57 theorem is

\[
\ker\Omega_V=\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf1,P_x|_V,P_y|_V\},
\qquad
\operatorname{rank}R_{C\cup H}[V]=2v-3,
\]

so `e+h>=2v-2` and `h>=delta>=ceil(D)` in the core ledger. This is not a full-source affine-kernel theorem. Contact-isolated coordinates add zero stress rows/columns and independent kernel directions. `C-ZRF-FULL-SOURCE` is rejected.

## Canonical state through audit.59

Audit.59 controls theorem authority.

All audit.52--58 results remain canonical.

Audit.59 adds \`C-ADAPTIVE-STRICT-SAFE-SATURATION\`.

Let \(R\) be any finite deletion set and let
\[
S\subset\operatorname{int}K
\]
be any bounded measurable region satisfying
\[
|y-a|>1
\qquad
(y\in S,\ a\in A\setminus R).
\]

Then
\[
\boxed{
E_C(R)=\varnothing
\Longrightarrow
\operatorname{area}S\le a_0|R|,
}
\]
and
\[
\boxed{
E_C(R)\ne\varnothing
\Longrightarrow
\operatorname{area}S\le a_0(|R|-1),
\qquad
a_0=\frac{\sqrt3}{2}.
}
\]

The theorem is adaptive: \(R\) may be chosen after inspecting the source geometry.

For a single contact-active vertex \(v\),
\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{int}K
\subseteq
\bigcup_{a\in A\setminus\{v\}}
\overline B(a,1).
}
\]

So deleting one contact-active vertex never opens a positive-area region strictly farther than one from every retained source point.

More generally, deleting contact-wall guards cannot generate net strict-safe triangular-lattice area credit. If \(Z\) is a contact-isolated population and \(B\) is a nonempty contact-active guard set, then every strict-safe region relative to \(Z\cup B\) has
\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{area}S
\le
a_0(|Z|+|B|-1).
}
\]

## Exact remaining split

### Full-core branch

Audit.58 provides exact finite motions that can reduce upper-diameter constraints to lower blockers or uncovered anchors. Audit.59 now says those obstructions cannot be repaired merely by deleting contact-active guards and averaging a slightly expanded triangular lattice over the recovered strict-safe area.

A successful repair must instead use one of:

1. an explicit finite packet whose capacity is certified discretely rather than by mean area;
2. a non-strict packing using exact unit contacts;
3. a collective exchange that recreates enough contact structure while deleting the diameter cage;
4. a finite non-repacking source motion that resolves the lower blockers/anchors;
5. an exact unit equilateral triangle.

Thus “break contacts, recover collar area, average a strict lattice” is a dead route.

### Macroscopic-container branch

The same barrier applies. Mean strict-safe area cannot fund the diameter cover after adaptive wall deletion. Any successful use of audit.54 refill must certify actual discrete packing capacity, not infer it from area alone.

Audit.57's short-wall obstruction and audit.54's large-wall lock regression remain binding.

